.he "mknod (1)                    8/12/26                    mknod (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
mknod
- make a device node
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
mknod name b/c major minor
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Mknod makes a special file called name, which is how a device is
reached through the filesystem.
.sp
The second argument is the kind of device: b for a block device, one
the system talks to a buffer at a time and can hold a filesystem, or c
for a character device, one it talks to a byte at a time.
.sp
Major is which driver, and minor is which unit that driver is to use -
which drive, which port.  Both are numbers, and their meanings belong
to the driver rather than to mknod.
.sp
Only the super-user may make a device node.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
EXAMPLE
.in +5
.br
.nf
     mknod /dev/tty1 c 0 1
     mknod /dev/hd0 b 2 0
.fi
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The one that came with micronix was broken.  This is a rewrite.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
ls (1)
